  "source_statement": {
    "english": "For every k >= 2 there exists c_k > 0 such that infinitely many n satisfy: the largest prime factor of each of n+1, ..., n+k exceeds n^{c_k}.",
    "chinese_agrees": true,
    "both_complete_guides_read": true,
    "domains": "Natural k and positive integral n, real exponent. Restricting witnesses to n>=2 is stronger and avoids the irrelevant boundary definitions at 0 and 1.",
    "interpretation": "The explicit largest-prime-factor condition is authoritative; the label rough adds no least-prime-factor requirement."
  },
  "semantic_findings": [
    {
      "requirement": "All k >= 2",
      "finding": "Statement universally quantifies k : Nat with 2 <= k; conjecture has no additional hypothesis or restriction."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "One fixed positive real exponent for each k",
      "finding": "The chosen c is the real inverse of the natural product 2*k after casting to Real. Its value is 1/(2k)>0. This choice precedes the infinite-set assertion and is independent of N, n and i."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "Actual greatest prime divisor",
      "finding": "largestPrimeFactor is the finite supremum of Nat.primeFactors with the standard Nat lattice. largestPrimeFactor_spec proves primality, divisibility and domination of every prime divisor for all m>=2. Nonemptiness follows since only 0 and 1 have empty primeFactors. threshold_iff_prime_divisor proves an exact equivalence, not merely a one-sided surrogate."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "All shifts and one common n",
      "finding": "Good quantifies every natural i with 1<=i<=k inside the predicate for one fixed n and c. CRT simultaneously provides divisibility for j=0,...,k-1; the formal conversion j=i-1 proves i-1+1=i. Both offsets 1 and k are present."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "Same base and strict real bound",
      "finding": "The full elaborated Good body uses Real.instPow = Real.rpow, Real.instLT and Real.instNatCast, with base n for every shift. The inequality is strictly n^c < P(n+i). No floor, natural division, altered base or non-strict replacement occurs."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "Ordinary infinitude",
      "finding": "Set.Infinite is the negation of Set.Finite on the subtype of qualifying naturals. unbounded_good produces n>N for every natural bound N; the final theorem uses Set.infinite_iff_exists_gt. Repeated witnesses or an infinite certificate index do not substitute for distinct unbounded naturals."
    },
    {
      "requirement": "Standard operations and no shadowed meaning",
      "finding": "Fully explicit printed definitions and theorem types use the standard Nat/Real types and instances. Nat.instDvd means exists c, b=a*c; Nat.prime_def supplies the usual prime criterion. No custom instance or notation is authored. A reviewer-authored SemanticSurface.lean successfully restates the entire conclusion explicitly with Real.rpow, Nat.cast, Nat.add and Set.Infinite, and independently expands the largest-factor prime criterion."
    }
  ],
  "mathematical_assessment": [
    "Fix k>=2 and N. Let t=max(2^k,N+k+3), so t>k, t>N, t>=2 and 2^k<=t.",
    "Bertrand supplies primes p_j in (2^j*t,2^(j+1)*t]. If j<l, the upper bound on p_j is at most the lower endpoint for p_l, proving strict increase and hence pairwise coprimality.",
    "For j<k, p_j>t>k>=j+1 makes p_j-(j+1) the intended nonnegative residue. Finite CRT gives n<product p_j and p_j divides n+(j+1).",
    "The j=0 divisibility is crucial: n+1 is positive, so p_0<=n+1. Since p_0>t in the integers, n>=t>N. The construction therefore cannot remain bounded as the parameter grows.",
    "Each p_j<=2^(j+1)*t<=2^k*t<=t^2. Thus n<product p_j<=t^(2k). Positivity and the real reciprocal-power equivalence yield n^(1/(2k))<t<p_j.",
    "The actual greatest prime divisor of each n+j+1 is at least p_j. Every inequality is simultaneous for the same n, and the construction beyond arbitrary N proves ordinary infinitude with the single exponent 1/(2k)."
  ],
